<%# The keep control: the one per-visitor thing on an otherwise public page.
    Locals: painting, kept, count, autofocus

    It carries its own <turbo-frame> rather than only the contents, because a
    frame-targeted response has to hand back a matching frame id or Turbo reports
    missing content.

    `autofocus` is true ONLY on the write responses. Turbo replaces the whole
    frame on submit, which destroys the focused button and drops focus to <body>;
    autofocus puts it back, and focus landing on a toggle is also what makes a
    screen reader announce the new state. On the initial lazy GET it is false —
    a fragment arriving mid-scroll must not steal focus, which is the same reason
    there is no aria-live here.

    No glyph in the kept state. `✦` is the product's only ornament (DESIGN.md
    rule 6) and using it here put two of them a few centimetres apart meaning
    different things. The words carry the state instead, with the middle dot
    `.label__credit` already uses. %>
<%= turbo_frame_tag "keep_#{painting.id}" do %>
  <div class="keep">
    <%= button_to favorite_path(painting), method: (kept ? :delete : :post),
          form: { class: "keep__form" },
          class: "caps-link keep__toggle",
          autofocus: autofocus,
          aria: {
            pressed: kept,
            label: kept ? "Remove #{painting.title} from your collection"
                        : "Keep #{painting.title} in your collection"
          } do %><%= kept ? "Kept · Remove" : "Keep this" %><% end %>

    <%# Only once there is something to see. The countless door on /days covers
        discovery at zero; this one is the count climbing, which is the reward.

        `turbo_frame: "_top"` because a link inside a frame navigates the frame by
        default — without it, tapping this loads the whole collection page into a
        44px box and the reader goes nowhere. %>
    <% if count.positive? %>
      <%= link_to "#{count} kept →", collection_path, class: "caps-link keep__link",
            data: { turbo_frame: "_top" } %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
<% end %>
